      "a": "It is provided that,\nBand gap of the p-n photodiode$=2.8eV$\nWavelength, $\\lambda =6000nm=6\\times {{10}^{-6}}m$\nIt is known that,\nEnergy of a signal, $E=\\frac{hc}{\\lambda }$\nwhere,\n$h$ is the Planck\u2019s constant, $h=6.626\\times {{10}^{-34}}Js$\n$c$ is the speed of light, $c=3\\times {{10}^{8}}m{{s}^{-1}}$\n$\\Rightarrow E=\\frac{6.626\\times {{10}^{-34}}\\times 3\\times {{10}^{8}}}{6\\times {{10}^{-6}}}$\n$\\Rightarrow E=3.313\\times {{10}^{-34+8+6}}$\n$\\Rightarrow E=3.313\\times {{10}^{-20}}J$\nIt is known that, $1.6\\times {{10}^{-19}}J=1eV$\n$\\Rightarrow E=\\frac{3.313\\times {{10}^{-20}}}{1.6\\times {{10}^{-19}}}eV$\n$\\Rightarrow E=0.207eV$\nEnergy of the signal with wavelength $6000nm$is $0.207eV$.\nAs the energy of the signal is less than $2.8eV$(energy band gap of p-n photodiodes) the signal can\u2019t be detected.\nTherefore, the photodiode cannot detect the signal."
